The decision to use aftermarket glass often hinges on cost reduction, but the trade-off involves potential degradation in safety margins and sensor accuracy. If you prioritize long-term liability reduction and system reliability, specifying OEM parts is not just a preference but a technical necessity. For more details on integrating these hardware constraints into software-driven fleet strategies, you can explore resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com).

Ultimately, the choice between OEM and aftermarket glass is an engineering decision involving material science, optical physics, and mechanical retention dynamics. Ignoring these factors in favor of immediate cost savings introduces unknown variables into a safety-critical subsystem that should be treated with the same rigor as any other critical infrastructure component.
